// runtime/sfn/platform/fs_exec_mode_windows.sfn
//
// Windows sibling of `fs_exec_mode.sfn`, selected for a Windows target by
// the matching table in `runtime/capsule.toml`. See that module's header for the
// full rationale; the short version is that POSIX `X_OK` (1) is not a
// legal UCRT `_access` mode and passing it terminates the process.
//
// `F_OK` (0) — existence — is the correct Windows semantic, not a
// workaround. Windows has no execute permission bit on regular files:
// executability is a property of the image format and the file's
// extension, not of a mode bit the filesystem can be asked about. So
// "does this path exist" is the strongest question `_access` can
// answer here, and it is exactly what the four `fs.is_executable`
// callers want — each is locating the compiler's own binary next to
// itself (`check/engine.sfn:151`, `build/paths.sfn:119`,
// `cli/commands/test/pool.sfn:82`, `cli/commands/dev_det_sweep.sfn:312`).
//
// Note this makes `fs.is_executable` equivalent to `fs.exists` on
// Windows. That is a deliberate semantic narrowing, and it is why the
// mode lives here rather than being flattened to `0` for every target.
//
// Verified against MSVC 14.51.36231 / SDK 10.0.26100.0: a C program
// calling `_access(path, 0)` returns 0, and the same program calling
// `_access(path, 1)` exits `-1073740791` (`0xC0000409`) without
// returning (SFN-649).
fn _sfn_fs_access_exec_mode() -> i32 { return 0; }

export { _sfn_fs_access_exec_mode };
